### insert Primitive

The insert primitive will insert a value into a participant or the key of a participant. It will throw an error if the target participant or the key of the target participant is not an array.

```text
insert <value_participant> <target_participant> [keys] <position>
```
Syntax Structure:
- `insert`:
    * Type: *string*
    * This is the identifier for the instruction.
- `<value_participant>` 
    * Type: *string*
    * This is the name of the participant whose value will be inserted.
- `<target_participant>` 
    * Type: *string*
    * This is the name of the participant that the value will be inserted into.
- `[keys]` 
    * Type: *Array*
    * This is an array of strings, representing the key path to key value to insert.
    * For example, ["a","b"] will be target_participant["a"]["b"]
    * If the key is specified as [], it indicates that the value should be directly assigned to the participant.

#### Example 1:
Inserts the book participant into the basket participant at index 0. It expects basket to be an array.

```text
insert book basket [] 0
```

Pre-transformation state:
```text
    {
        book: {"name": "Harry Potter"},
        basket: [],
    }
```

Post-transformation state:
```text
    {
        book: {"name": "Harry Potter"},
        basket: [{"name": "Harry Potter"}],
    }
```

#### Example 2:
Inserts the book participant into the basket participant's `contents` key. It expets the contents key of the basket to be an array.

```text
insert book basket ["contents"] 0
```

Pre-transformation state:
```text
    {
        book: {"name": "Harry Potter"},
        basket: {"contents": []},
    }
```

Post-transformation state:
```text
    {
        book: {"name": "Harry Potter"},
        basket: {"contents": [{"name": "Harry Potter"}]},
    }
```